a) Without a domain — each computer manages its own users

Imagine you have 3 Windows computers:

PC-1
PC-2
PC-3

And

PC-1 → User accounts stored on PC-1
PC-2 → User accounts stored on PC-2
PC-3 → User accounts stored on PC-3

Suppose you create a user:

Username: bilal
Password: ******

on PC-1.

That user exists only on PC-1.

If you want Bilal to log in to PC-2, you would need to create another account on PC-2:

PC-1 → bilal
PC-2 → bilal
PC-3 → bilal

Each computer independently manages its own users and passwords.

b) With Domain

Administrator can create user account in form of domain.

Suppose your company wants to use:

company.local

You create a Windows domain:

company.local

Then users can have accounts such as:

bilal@company.local
ahmed@company.local
john@company.local

Computers can become members of this domain:

PC001
PC002
PC003
SERVER01
SERVER02

Now suppose you have a Windows Server running Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS).

For eg

              Windows Server
              Domain Controller
              example.local
                   |
        ┌──────────┼──────────┐
        ↓          ↓          ↓
       PC-1       PC-2       PC-3

You create the user bilal in Active Directory:

Username: bilal
Password: ******

The account is stored in the Active Directory database on the Domain Controller.

Now Bilal can use his domain account to log in to domain-joined computers:

PC-1 → bilal
PC-2 → bilal
PC-3 → bilal

You don't need to create a separate local Bilal account on every computer.

